  "result": "MobileVLA-R1: Reinforcing Vision-Language-Action for Mobile Robots (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17889v1)\nciteturn6727view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17889v1\",\"lineno\":128}); Total lines: 375\nL125:  | $$R_{m}=\\frac{\\mathbf{o^{\\prime}}^{\\top}\\mathbf{o^{\\prime*}}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{o^{\\prime}}\\rVert_{2}\\,\\lVert\\mathbf{o^{\\prime*}}\\rVert_{2}},$$  |  | (2)\nL126: \nL127: where $\\mathbf{o^{\\prime}}=(V_{x},V_{y},V_{yaw})$. This formulation promotes directionally aligned and dynamically consistent control, while discrete actions are supervised separately by the action reward.\nL128: Action reward. This reward supervises discrete control behaviors by checking whether the predicted action $a$ matches the ground-truth label $a^{*}$. It provides a simple yet effective signal for aligning high-level decisions with target actions:\nL129: \nL130:  | $$R_{action}=\\begin{cases}1,&\\text{if }a=a^{*}\\\\\nL131: 0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}.$$  |  | (3)\nL132: \nL133: Such a binary formulation offers clear feedback on action correctness and complements the continuous guidance from the movement reward.\nL134: Format reward. To ensure structural consistency, the format reward $R_{format}$ checks whether the model output strictly follows the reasoning–execution template <think>...</think><answer>...</answer>. Formally,\nL135: \nL136:  | $$R_{format}=\\begin{cases}1,&\\text{if output satisfies format}\\\\\nL137: 0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}.$$  |  | (4)\nL138: \nL139: This constraint guarantees machine-parseable outputs and enforces consistent mapping from reasoning traces to executable control commands.\nL140: Policy update. Given $N$ responses sampled from the current policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$, we compute individual rewards $\\mathbf{r}=(r_{1},r_{2},\\dots,r_{N})$ and advantages\nL141: \nL142:  | $$A_{i}=r_{i}-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{j=1}^{N}r_{j},$$  |  | (5)\nL143: \nL144: which are normalized as $\\hat{A}_{i}=\\frac{A_{i}}{\\sigma_{A}+\\epsilon}$ for stability. The policy is then optimized using the clipped GRPO objective:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16233v1)\nciteturn6727view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16233v1\",\"lineno\":116}); Total lines: 523\nL114: In our implementation, this guide model uses the exact same network architecture as the downstream policy model and is obtained by light, insufficient training on the original dataset (only 10%-20% of the total time required for the standard procedure). Its cost has been accounted for as part of our framework’s total overhead.\nL115: In our implementation, we use the LiSSA algorithm (cite66†Klochkov and Liu 2024 ) to efficiently approximate the Hessian-inverse-vector product (see Appendix for hyperparameter discussion). The influence score calculated by this method constitutes the base influence score of this engine, $Score_{base}(d_{i})$.\nL116: #### Stage 2: Contrastive Verification Refinement\nL117: \nL118: To further ensure that samples with a high $Score_{base}$ are indeed positively valued and contribute to generalization, we perform contrastive verification on the top-K% elite samples.\nL119: For each elite sample $d_{i}$, we generate a ”minimal counterexample” $d_{\\text{contrast}}$ using a Cross-Modal Mismatch strategy common in robot robustness evaluation. Specifically, while keeping the language ($L$) and action ($A$) sequences fixed, we programmatically modify the initial visual scene ($V$) in a simulator to create a new scene ($V^{\\prime}$) with semantic or physical contradictions, as illustrated in Figure cite73†2 .\nL120: Programmatic Counterexample Generation. This strategy’s automation and generalizability are rooted in the structured VLA task space. Since task categories are limited and classifiable (e.g.,  40 in the LIBERO dataset), we can pre-design a small set of Reusable Perturbation Templates. As detailed in Figure cite73†2 , for any given elite sample $d_{i}$, our script generates a counterexample $d_{\\text{contrast}}$ through a fully automated ”Template Matching and Instantiation” process.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAeroVerse: UAV-Agent Benchmark Suite for Simulating, Pre-training, Finetuning, and Evaluating Aerospace Embodied World Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2408.15511v2)\nciteturn6727view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2408.15511v2\",\"lineno\":320}); Total lines: 580\nL319: This stage reinforces the model’s spatial modeling and causal reasoning capabilities through complex questions in the SkyAgent-Reason3k dataset, laying the groundwork for subsequent navigation tasks. The Aerospace Embodied Navigational Exploration task requires the drone to explore the environment based on long-distance navigation instructions (e.g., “Fly forward 200 meters and then turn left”) and answer attribute-related questions about objects encountered along the way.\nL320: The navigation task introduces a temporal dimension and action sequences, requiring the model to translate the perception and reasoning abilities learned in the previous stages into concrete actions. Through training on the SkyAgent-Nav3k dataset, the model learns to achieve a preliminary “perception-reasoning-action” loop in complex urban environments.\nL321: The Aerospace Embodied Task Planning task is positioned as the advanced planning stage in the curriculum. This task requires the drone to generate detailed path plans (e.g., “First go straight to the red building, then turn right and bypass the tall building”) based on the starting and ending points. This stage is an extension of the navigation task but places greater emphasis on global planning capabilities.\nL322: ### VII-C SkyAgent & SkyAgentX\nL323: As illustrated in Figure cite96†12 , based on a pre-trained general vision-language model Internvl-2.5-8B[cite97†39 ], we introduce the aerospace embodied chain-of-thought mechanism and fine-tune it using specialized instruction datasets (i.e., SkyAgent-Scene3k, SkyAgent-Reason3k, SkyAgent-Nav3k, and SkyAgent-Plan3k) for four downstream tasks, to develop the SkyAgent model.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBehind the Screens: Uncovering Bias in AI-Driven Video Interview Assessments Using Counterfactuals (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2)\nciteturn6727view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2\",\"pattern\":\"InterFaceGAN\"}); Total lines: 541\nL207: To address this, we use the In-Domain GAN Inversion approach by Zhu et al. [cite104†69 ]. This method offers two advantages: (i) the use of a domain-guided encoder to invert images, ensuring all codes are in-domain (i.e., they also encode semantic knowledge) for better editing, and (ii) the use of instance-based domain-regularized optimization for image reconstruction, which supports starting at an ideal point to avoid local minima and create more realistic images [cite104†69 ].\nL208: Thus, for each image $\\mathbf{x}$, instead of the standard generation method where $\\mathbf{x}=G(\\mathbf{z})$ and $\\mathbf{z}$ is the corresponding latent code for the image, Zhu et al.’s inversion method inputs $\\mathbf{x}$ to an encoder $E$, resulting in $\\mathbf{z}^{inv}$ for modification [cite104†69 ].\nL209: For image modification, we use the approach by Shen et al. [cite105†70 ], Interpreting Face GAN (InterFaceGAN). In this approach, attribute boundary vectors $\\alpha$ are inferred from a GAN’s latent codes and corresponding image attribute annotations.\nL210: Therefore, for any obtained latent code $\\mathbf{z}^{inv}$, a new image is reconstructed by $\\mathbf{x}^{rec}=G(\\mathbf{z}^{inv}+\\lambda\\alpha)$, wherein $\\alpha$ is the learned boundary and $\\lambda\\in[-\\infty,\\infty]$ is the direction of attribute modification (as $\\lambda$ increases, the attribute prominence increases, and as $\\lambda$ decreases, the attribute prominence decreases) [cite105†70 , cite55†23 ]. For our experiments, we use StyleGAN boundaries trained on the FFHQ dataset for gender and age.\nL211: We also train our own boundary for ethnicity by grouping images into categories of majority and minority groups. Lastly, a quality boundary is used and applied after each attribute modification to remove image artifacts; we note that this boundary, provided by InterFaceGAN, was trained on the CelebA-HQ library.\nL212: ## IV Ethical Considerations\nL213: ### IV-A Intended Use\nL214: The framework outlined in this paper is intended for analyzing visual biases in video-based predictor models. Our framework uses the OCEAN model, which is commonly applied in hiring and marketing contexts, and has been extensively tested and widely adopted in deep learning applications [cite37†7 ]. However, we caution against the use of other personality models, such as Myers-Briggs, which, although used in some predictive systems, have been criticized for lacking empirical validity [cite47†17 ].\nL215: We also caution against applying this framework to other affective computing tasks, such as facial emotion inference, which has been shown to be unreliable due to cultural variation in emotional expression and the influence of social context and emotional masking [cite48†18 ]. Applying fairness auditing methods in such settings risks reinforcing invalid assumptions, as the underlying data and task definitions may already encode societal bias.\nL216: Additionally, while our counterfactual modification framework can reveal and help quantify model biases, it is not intended as a prescriptive tool for real-time hiring decisions. We also emphasize that the protected attribute classifier used in this study serves only to evaluate the success of counterfactual generation. It is not used for any downstream prediction, selection, or inference task, and should not be repurposed for such applications.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBehind the Screens: Uncovering Bias in AI-Driven Video Interview Assessments Using Counterfactuals (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2)\nciteturn6727view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2\",\"pattern\":\"III\"}); Total lines: 541\nL138: This is done in two ways: first, by selecting the top $N$ candidates based on their interview score, and second, by selecting only candidates with an interview score above an $\\epsilon$ threshold. Then, we use the metric of Disparate Impact (previously described in Sec. cite10†II-A ), which is based on hiring legislation, to quantify selection bias.\nL139: Lastly, in Experiment 3, we evaluate our method for counterfactual generation by training a separate model for predicting job candidates’ protected attributes. We use this to assess the effectiveness of our counterfactuals, where a decline in the protected attribute model’s accuracy after attribute manipulation indicates successful changes to the individuals’ protected attributes. We also highlight our intent for the ethical use of this model and our overall framework in Sec. cite21†IV .\nL140: Our model architecture, counterfactual facial attribute manipulation approach, and dataset used are described next.\nL141: ### III-A Dataset\nL142: We use the CLFI dataset [cite43†13 , cite44†14 ], one of the largest datasets available for personality prediction [cite37†7 ]. It contains 10,000 videos of individuals speaking to a camera, each 15 seconds long, with labels for their OCEAN personality trait scores and a job interview score (i.e., the likelihood of being invited back for an interview). We refer to all these scores collectively as OCEANI, where I represents the interview score.\nL143: These labels, obtained through Mechanical Turk and pairwise comparisons between videos, represent first impressions. Each individual’s scores for each OCEANI dimension range from 0 to 1, with 1 being the highest score and 0 the lowest. The distribution of this dataset among the protected attribute groups is illustrated in Table cite81†I . While the gender attribute is fairly balanced across the dataset, there is an imbalance in ethnicity and age, which may contribute to bias.\nL144: We note that for the age attribute, we remove videos without an age label when analyzing bias. For the remaining videos, we group individuals into two age categories based on legal protection under the Age Discrimination in Employment Act (ADEA) [cite82†49 ]: those aged 40 or older (protected group) and those under 40 (reference group).\nL145: TABLE I: Dataset Distribution Per Protected Attribute Group, Before and After Filtering. The first row (“Before”) contains videos from the original dataset. The second row (“After”) contains videos after filtering out those in which individuals were not facing the camera and could not undergo counterfactual facial manipulation.\nL146: Demographic groups are abbreviated as follows: Female (F), Male (M), Caucasian (Cau.), African-American (Af.Am.), Asian (Asi.), individuals at or above the age of 40 ($\\text{A}\\geq 40$), and individuals below the age of 40 ($\\text{A}<40$).\nL147: Stage  | Gender  | Ethnicity  | Age\nL148: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL149:  | F  | M  | Af.Am.  | Cau.  | Asi.  | $\\text{A}<40$  | $\\text{A}\\geq 40$\nL150: Before  | 5,462  | 4,538  | 1,071  | 8,598  | 331  | 8,214  | 384\nL151: After  | 3,916  | 2,764  | 842  | 5,572  | 266  | 5,346  | 226\nL152: cite83†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 2: Architecture of the multi-task deep neural network model (MTDNN model) [cite84†50 ] used for personality and interview score prediction.\nL153: ### III-B Personality Prediction Models\nL154: Our experiments utilize two models. The first is a multi-task deep neural network (MTDNN) model based on the state-of-the-art architecture described by Mujtaba and Mahapatra [cite84†50 ], and is used in Experiments 1 and 2. The second is a multi-task model for predicting protected attributes, which we refer to as the P-Attribute model, and is used in Experiment 3. The MTDNN model and its variants follow a training procedure and architecture similar to that described in [cite84†50 ].\nL155: An overview of both models, including their architecture and training procedure, is provided next.\nL156: #### III-B 1 OCEANI predictor model (MTDNN model)\nL157: The architecture of our OCEANI predictor model, or MTDNN model, is depicted in Figure cite85†2 . This model is a multi-task neural network where all inputs are combined as input to shared hidden layers. Each OCEANI score then has its own task-specific layer to output final predictions. The advantage of this method is its ability to share information across the shared layers and optimize task-specific predictions at the task-specific layers.\nL158: Moreover, this reduces training time, as only one model is needed for training, rather than separate models for each score and feature extraction. Additionally, information learned from video features such as the face, scene, audio, and text is used to infer all OCEAN traits and the interview score.\nL159: Overall, the features input to our model consist of face, scene, audio, and text features extracted from interview videos. These are modeled after the BU-NKU model feature extraction method, presented by Kaya et al. [cite44†14 ], for our approach. First, faces of individuals in the videos are aligned using IntraFace, and facial features are extracted using a pretrained VGG-Face network [cite86†51 , cite44†14 ].\nL160: Second, scene features (i.e., the individual’s surroundings in the video) are extracted using the VGG-VD-19 network, a model trained for object recognition that detects object-like parts in the first frame of the video [cite44†14 , cite45†15 ]. Third, deep facial features are compared and combined for effective emotion recognition using a spatio-temporal descriptor, Local Gabor Binary Patterns from Three Orthogonal Planes (LGBP-TOP), which has been shown to be effective for models to predict emotions [cite44†14 ].\nL169: #### III-B 2 Protected attribute predictor model (P-Attribute model)\nL174: This model was also implemented in PyTorch, following a similar training procedure to our MTDNN model, with modifications for multi-class classification. We also use five-fold cross-validation for training. However, since the number of individuals per minority group is lower than that of the majority group, we modify our loss function to the following unreduced cross-entropy loss $l(x,y)=L=\\{l_{1},\\dots,l_{N}\\}^{T}$, where the loss for every $n$th item in the batch of size $N$ is:\nL175:  | $$l=-\\sum^{C}_{c=1}w_{c}\\log\\frac{e^{x_{n,c}}}{\\sum^{C}_{i=1}e^{x_{n,i}}}y_{n,c},$$  |  | (4)\nL176: where $x$ is the input image, $y$ is the target label, $C$ is the total number of classes, and $w$ is a weight vector for individual weights applied per item in the batch. For both the ethnicity and age categories, we apply weights according to the ratio of the number of individuals in the majority group to the number of individuals in the minority group. For gender, both groups were weighted equally since there was a nearly equal number of records for both groups.\nL177: ### III-C Counterfactual Video Generation\nL178: Our counterfactual video generation process contains four stages. First, we extract each video’s frames and align the faces of each individual to the center of the image using Python’s dlib library [cite94†59 ] and the FFHQ pre-trained face extraction model [cite95†60 ].\nL179: From each frame, we produce $256\\times 256$ and $64\\times 64$ pixel images, with the $64\\times 64$ images being used for feature extraction and model training, and the $256\\times 256$ images used for modification, which are later resized to $64\\times 64$ images. For our MTDNN model, we extract facial, movement, and scenic features from these images using the procedure described by Kaya et al. [cite44†14 ].\nL180: Next, to synthesize modified image frames, we invert each frame image back to our GAN’s latent space (i.e., the GAN’s search space of latent codes generated from a fixed prior distribution used during training). The GAN model and inversion method we use are further described later. Afterwards, the inverted images, which map to our GAN model’s latent space, can be modified along a learned semantic boundary (e.g., gender or ethnicity).\nL181: These modifications target a single set of attributes that map along a direction in the latent space, which is advantageous because unrelated attributes remain unchanged during image modification. Finally, the modified image frames are used as counterfactuals for all our experiments.\nL182: #### III-C 1 Generative adversarial networks\nL183: Generative adversarial networks (GANs) are a powerful framework for generative modeling and have been used to create models to generate images, sound, and text [cite96†61 , cite97†62 ]. In GANs, two models compete during training: a generator and a discriminator. The generator, $G$, creates data samples $x$ that aim to be indistinguishable from real data based on the training data distribution $p_{g}$ [cite97†62 ].\nL184: The generator competes against an adversary, the discriminator, $D$, that classifies these samples as either real (i.e., part of the original dataset) or fake (i.e., generated by $G$) [cite96†61 ].\nL185: An input noise $p_{z}(z)$ from the data space $G(z;\\theta_{g})$, where $\\theta_{g}$ are model parameters, is fed into the generator. The discriminator, $D$, is trained to maximize the accuracy of $D(x;\\theta_{d})$, the probability that the data sample $x$ is real and not generated by $G$. Simultaneously, $G$ is trained to minimize $\\log(1-D(G(z)))$, reducing the discriminator’s ability to correctly classify the generated data. This results in the following optimization problem [cite97†62 ]:\nL186:  | $$\\begin{split}\\min_{G}\\max_{D}V(D,G)=\\ &\\mathbf{E}_{x\\sim p_{data}(x)}[\\log D(x)]\\ +\\\\\nL187: &\\mathbf{E}_{z\\sim p_{z}(z)}[\\log(1-D(G(z)))],\\end{split}$$  |  | (5)\nL188: \nL189: where $p_{data}$ is the distribution of the training data.\nL190: \nL191: GANs have many advantages over standard generative models, including the ability to generate highly realistic images. For our approach, we use StyleGAN to modify video interviews.\nL192: #### III-C 2 StyleGAN\nL193: StyleGAN is a GAN architecture introduced in 2019 that re-designs the generator model to include mapping and style networks, resulting in the generation of photo-realistic and high-quality images [cite95†60 ]. It has been used for image-to-image translation [cite98†63 ], face generation [cite99†64 ], and in medical applications [cite100†65 ].\nL194: In contrast to traditional GAN models, where only a single point from the latent space is given as input, StyleGAN adds two additional sources of randomness: a mapping neural network and noise layers. First, a latent code $\\mathbf{z}$ is given from the latent space $\\mathbf{z}\\in\\mathcal{Z}$ [cite95†60 , cite101†66 ]. This code is then input to StyleGAN’s mapping neural network $f$, an 8-layer MLP, which outputs $\\mathbf{w}\\in\\mathcal{W}$, an intermediate latent space [cite95†60 , cite101†66 ].\nL195: The vector $\\mathbf{w}$ is input to the StyleGAN generator model, where spatially invariant styles $y=(y_{s},y_{b})$ can be computed for each image, with $y_{s}$ used for scaling and $y_{b}$ for style transformation as bias [cite95†60 , cite101†66 ]. The generator model in StyleGAN provides control over fine details and has the capability to create more realistic images due to the intermediate latent space and its unique architecture.\nL196: Between each convolution layer in the generator network, a control adaptive instance normalization (AdaIN) operation is applied, as described by [cite95†60 ]:\nL197: \nL198:  | $$\\text{AdaIN}(x_{i},y)=y_{s,i}\\frac{x_{i}-\\mu(x_{i})}{\\sigma(x_{i})}+y_{b,i},$$  |  | (6)\nL199: \nL200: where $x_{i}$ is a feature map normalized separately and scaled with the style from $y$, supporting style transfer.\nL201: In 2020, StyleGAN was extended to StyleGAN2, significantly improving the quality of generated images by replacing normalization in the generator model with demodulation, thereby removing characteristic artifacts [cite101†66 , cite102†67 ].\nL202: For our work, we use the StyleGAN2 implementation in PyTorch [cite102†67 ], pre-trained on the Flickr-Faces-HQ dataset, which contains 1024x1024 resolution facial images.^{2}^{2} 2 https://github.com/NVlabs/ffhq-dataset Although StyleGAN2 was further extended to StyleGAN3 in 2021, we use StyleGAN2 for all experiments due to its superior capability for style mixing.\nL203: Style mixing was disabled in StyleGAN3 training due to the focus of the model, which resulted in poorer performance for our purposes compared to StyleGAN2 [cite103†68 ].\nL204: #### III-C 3 Attribute manipulation\nL205: To modify each individual frame of a video, we first need to map the image to StyleGAN’s latent space, a process known as GAN inversion. This is challenging due to the size and complexity of a GAN’s latent space, where standard optimization methods (e.g., gradient descent) for searching a latent code are too time-consuming and impractical. Therefore, existing methods train an encoder to map images to latent codes and a decoder to reconstruct the original image [cite104†69 ].\nL206: However, past work has mainly focused on reconstruction using solely image pixels, leaving out semantic knowledge encoded in the latent space that would assist in more accurate image editing.\nL207: To address this, we use the In-Domain GAN Inversion approach by Zhu et al. [cite104†69 ]. This method offers two advantages: (i) the use of a domain-guided encoder to invert images, ensuring all codes are in-domain (i.e., they also encode semantic knowledge) for better editing, and (ii) the use of instance-based domain-regularized optimization for image reconstruction, which supports starting at an ideal point to avoid local minima and create more realistic images [cite104†69 ].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Analysis of Large Language Model Inference Serving Systems: A Performance Study of vLLM and HuggingFace TGI (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17593v1)\nciteturn6727view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17593v1\",\"pattern\":\"Experimental\"}); Total lines: 573\nL96: The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews related work on LLM serving systems. Section 3 describes our experimental methodology. Section 4 presents detailed performance results across multiple dimensions. Section 5 analyzes the architectural factors underlying observed differences. Section 6 discusses practical implications, and Section 7 concludes.\nL117: ### 2.3 Benchmarking Studies\nL118: \nL119: While several benchmarking efforts exist, they often have limited scope. Prior work has examined individual aspects such as memory efficiency or single-model performance, but comprehensive comparisons across multiple dimensions remain scarce. Our work extends previous efforts by providing systematic evaluation across model sizes, workload patterns, and performance metrics relevant to production deployment decisions.\nL120: \nL121: ## 3 Methodology\nL122: \nL123: ### 3.1 Experimental Setup\nL555:   * [8] Aminabadi, R. Y., Rajbhandari, S., Zhang, M., Awan, A. A., Li, C., Li, D., … & He, Y. (2022). DeepSpeed inference: Enabling efficient inference of transformer models at unprecedented scale. Proceedings of the International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis, 1-15.\nL556:   * [9] Sheng, Y., Zheng, L., Yuan, B., Li, Z., Ryabinin, M., Chen, B., … & Stoica, I. (2023). FlexGen: High-throughput generative inference of large language models with a single GPU. arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.06865.\nL557:   * [10] Zheng, L., Chiang, W. L., Sheng, Y., Zhuang, S., Wu, Z., Zhuang, Y., … & Stoica, I. (2023). Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-bench and chatbot arena. arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.05685.\nL558: Experimental support, please cite50†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite51†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL559: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL560: \nL561: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL562: \nL563:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL564: \nL565: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL566: Our team has already identified cite52†the following issues†github.com . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL567: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a cite53†list of packages that need conversion†github.com , and welcome cite54†developer contributions†github.com .\nL568: \nL569: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite55†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detectionfor Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1)\nciteturn6727view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1\",\"pattern\":\"3.1\"}); Total lines: 276\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 Methodology L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Problem Formulation L20:     2. cite10†3.2 Hierarchical Detection Framework L21:     3. cite11†3.3 Implementation Details L22:   5. cite12†4 Experiments L23:     1. cite13†4.1 Experimental Setup L24:     2. cite14†4.2 Results L25:     3. cite15†4.3 Cross-Benchmark Consistency L26:     4. cite16†4.4 Performance-Cliff Analysis L27:   6. cite17†5 Discussion L28:     1. cite18†5.1 Implications for Responsible Synthetic Data L29:     2. cite19†5.2 Limitations and Future Work L30:     3. cite20†5.3 Broader Impact L31:   7. cite21†6 Conclusion L32:   8. cite22†References L33:   9. cite23†A Additional Experimental Details L34:     1. cite24†A.1 Hyperparameter Selection L35:     2. cite25†A.2 Computational Costs L36:     3. cite26†A.3 Human Expert Evaluation Protocol L37:   10. cite27†B Algorithm Pseudocode L38: cite28†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L39: \nL40: arXiv:2511.17602v1 [cs.LG] 18 Nov 2025\nL41: # Beyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detection\nL42: for Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models\nL43: \nL44: Sushant Mehta\nL45: ###### Abstract\nL46: Synthetic data has become essential for training foundation models, yet benchmark contamination threatens evaluation integrity. Although existing detection methods identify token-level overlap, they fail to detect semantic-level contamination where synthetic data conceptually resemble benchmarks without lexical overlap. This gap is critical as foundation models increasingly train on synthetic data that may implicitly encode benchmark knowledge.\nL66: Dynamic Benchmarking. Recognizing contamination challenges, recent work proposes continuously updated benchmarks. LiveBench (cite45†White et al. 2024 ) releases monthly questions from recent sources, achieving contamination limitation through temporal cutoffs. However, temporal approaches face limitations: problems from coding competitions are “likely to be reused in future competitions” (cite46†Wu et al. 2024 ), and continuous updates require significant human effort.\nL67: Our framework complements dynamic benchmarking by enabling detection of contamination even in regularly refreshed datasets.\nL68: ## 3 Methodology\nL69: ### 3.1 Problem Formulation\nL70: \nL71: Let $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{syn}}=\\{x_{i}^{s}\\}_{i=1}^{N_{s}}$ denote a synthetic training dataset and $\\mathcal{B}=\\{x_{j}^{b}\\}_{j=1}^{N_{b}}$ a benchmark test set. We define contamination at severity level $\\ell\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ as:\nL72:  | $$\\text{Contaminated}_{\\ell}(x_{i}^{s},\\mathcal{B})=\\begin{cases}\\exists x_{j}^{b}:\\text{TokenMatch}(x_{i}^{s},x_{j}^{b})&\\ell=1\\\\\nL73: \\exists x_{j}^{b}:\\text{Semantic}(x_{i}^{s},x_{j}^{b})>\\tau_{s}&\\ell=2\\\\\nL74: \\exists x_{j}^{b}:\\text{ConceptCluster}(x_{i}^{s},x_{j}^{b})&\\ell=3\\\\\nL75: \\exists x_{j}^{b}:\\text{ReasonPattern}(x_{i}^{s},x_{j}^{b})&\\ell=4\\end{cases}$$  |  | (1)\nL76: where $\\tau_{s}$ is a semantic similarity threshold. The challenge is that contamination at level 3 (semantic) and level 4 (reasoning pattern) evades existing detection methods despite inflating evaluation metrics.\nL77: ### 3.2 Hierarchical Detection Framework\nL78: \nL79: Our framework processes synthetic training data through four hierarchical levels, with each level designed to catch contamination types missed by previous levels.\nL80: Level 1: Token Level Detection We employ Min-K% Prob (cite41†Deng et al. 2024b ) to identify pretraining data without access to the training corpus. For each test sample $x_{j}^{b}$, we compute token-level logarithmic probabilities $\\{\\log p(t_{k}|t_{<k})\\}_{k=1}^{|x_{j}^{b}|}$ from the target model. The Min-K% score aggregates the lowest K% of probabilities:\nL81: \nL82:  | $$\\text{Score}_{\\text{MinK}}(x_{j}^{b})=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k\\in\\text{bottom-K\\%}}\\log p(t_{k}|t_{<k})$$  |  | (2)\n"
}
